@meta
id: 04-07
title: Counting upwards as well
chapter: 04
type: lesson
skills: hjkl, count-prefix, relativenumber
par: 62
minutes: 6
vscode: scroll down, click, scroll back up, click again
open: runbook.md
vimrc: set relativenumber number

@brief
`k` takes a count exactly like `j` does, and the margin gives you the
number for it just as readily — the lines above the cursor count upwards
from 1 as well.

This runbook needs two additions, and they are deliberately in the wrong
order for you:

  1. The last line of the file, which currently ends `incident doc`,
     should read `incident doc and link the batch id.`

  2. The paragraph under **Retrying a failed batch** ends with
     `already settled.` Append ` Owner: payments.` to that line — a
     space, then the words.

Do the last line first, then come back up for the second. That upward
jump is the lesson.

Thirty-two lines is already past the point where an arrow key is
defensible: down, down, down, overshoot, back up, and a second edit
means doing it all again in reverse. Two counts do the whole thing.

Both `relativenumber` and `number` are on here, so the cursor line shows
its real line number and everything else shows a distance. That pairing
is what most people run with.

@keys
| `31j` | down thirty-one lines |
| `9k` | up nine lines |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
Read the distance to the last line off the margin, jump, and make the
first edit.
--
After that edit the margin has renumbered around your new position. Read
it again — do not reuse the number you read a moment ago.
--
From the bottom, the `already settled.` line is nine lines up:
`9k`, then `A`.

@solution
    31j A  and link the batch id. <Esc>
    9k A  Owner: payments. <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

The interesting keystrokes are `31j` and `9k`. Everything between them
is typing, which vim cannot help you with.

The habit this is building is: look, read the margin, jump, edit, look
again. Beginners coming from an editor with a mouse tend to move in a
straight line down a file and then scroll back if they missed something,
because scrolling back is expensive. It is not expensive here. A jump
back up costs the same as a jump down, so you can work in whatever order
the job actually wants.

Note that the file scrolled under you rather than the cursor running off
the bottom of the screen. Vim keeps the cursor where you put it and moves
the text — you will never again need to think about the scroll position
as a separate thing.

@start runbook.md
# Payments runbook

## Who to call

The payments on-call rota is in the team handbook. Escalate to the
platform team only after the provider status page has been checked.

## Symptoms

Charges time out after thirty seconds. The gateway returns 504 and the
order is left in PENDING.

## First checks

1. Provider status page
2. Our own error rate in the dashboard
3. The webhook queue depth
4. The retry worker logs

## Retrying a failed batch

Run the reconcile task with the batch id. It is safe to run twice; the
task is idempotent and skips anything already settled.

## Known false alarms

A spike in PENDING orders at the top of the hour is the scheduled export
holding a lock for about forty seconds. It clears on its own.

## After the incident

Write the timeline up in the incident doc

@expected runbook.md
# Payments runbook

## Who to call

The payments on-call rota is in the team handbook. Escalate to the
platform team only after the provider status page has been checked.

## Symptoms

Charges time out after thirty seconds. The gateway returns 504 and the
order is left in PENDING.

## First checks

1. Provider status page
2. Our own error rate in the dashboard
3. The webhook queue depth
4. The retry worker logs

## Retrying a failed batch

Run the reconcile task with the batch id. It is safe to run twice; the
task is idempotent and skips anything already settled. Owner: payments.

## Known false alarms

A spike in PENDING orders at the top of the hour is the scheduled export
holding a lock for about forty seconds. It clears on its own.

## After the incident

Write the timeline up in the incident doc and link the batch id.
